# The rules of CDNF, written in CDNF: this file reads as it says.

:cdnf:

The Colon Dash and Newline Format is natural text a program can read
too. A plain message is already a document, and its few marks are the
ones people write by hand anyway, colons, dashes and line ends.

Everything is text. A value is never a number, nor a true or a false,
only the text it is written as, and what it means is its reader's to
say.

::elements::

Every element is one of three kinds, and nothing else.

kinds :
- a text body
- a pair : what comes before a colon, and the element after it
-- a list : items, each opened by a dash

::text bodies::

A text body runs on until a blank line, or until another element
starts. One line end inside it is part of it, so this body is one
text over three lines.

Spaces at a line's start and end mean nothing, so a body may be
indented however it reads best.

Any run of empty lines is one blank line. A line holding only a
comment is no line at all, and parts nothing.

::pairs::

A colon anywhere but at a line's start makes a pair, of what came
before it and the next element after it, whatever kind that is.

a pair : its right

a pair of a pair : left : right

a pair of a list :
- the list's first item
-- its last item

The right may start on the line after the colon, even after a blank
line.

A text on the line right after a pair's text right is more of that
text, so pairs one after another are parted by blank lines, or made a
list's items.

::lists::

A dash at a line's start opens a list's item, and two dashes open its
last one, after which the list has ended. A list with no last item
ends at whatever comes after a blank line and is no item. A line
right after an item's text, with no dash, is more of that text.

A list nested in another ends on two dashes, or the outer list's next
items would join it.

nesting :
- an item
- an item holding a list :
    - an inner item
    -- the inner list's last item
- back in the outer list
-- two lists ending at once :
    -- the inner list's one item

Spaces around a dash or a colon mean nothing. Indentation is for the
eye alone, nested lists a step deeper.

::sections::

A line of colons, a name, and as many colons again is a section's
line. One colon on each side opens a section of the document, two a
subsection of the one before, and so on. Its colons start the line,
with no space before them.

a section's line : "::name::"

A section holds its own elements first, then its subsections. The
elements before any section are the document's own.

::quotes::

Where an element starts, a quote opens a quoted text, running to the
next quote. Everything between is kept as written, colons, dashes,
hashes and blank lines too. A backslash before a quote or another
backslash escapes it.

a quoted text : "colons : and - dashes # stay
as they are,

blank lines too, and a \"quote\" escaped"

A quote anywhere else is text, like the ones in he said "hi". After a
closing quote, only a colon or the line's end may come.

::comments::

A hash opens a comment, running to its line's end, and it means
nothing.   # like this one
